WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.119 --> 00:00:01.140
Good morning Hank, it's Tuesday.

00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:04.500
I had oral surgery yesterday, so I'm in a
lot of pain, but I'm also on a lot of pain

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:08.389
medication, so I apologize if I slur my words
or if blood dribbles out of the corner of

00:00:08.389 --> 00:00:09.389
my mouth or whatever.

00:00:09.389 --> 00:00:12.450
But anyway, Hank, today's video is like the
Buddhist path to enlightenment: it comes to

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:13.469
you in eight parts.

00:00:13.469 --> 00:00:16.180
So Hank, later this week there's gonna be
this big meeting in New York City, in which

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:21.780
193 world leaders will agree to some global
goals for humans to achieve by 2030.

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:25.980
A while back, a similar meeting happened at
which world leaders agreed to the Millennium

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:30.250
Development Goals: eight goals to change the
history of the world forever.

00:00:30.250 --> 00:00:32.940
And surprisingly enough, it sort of worked.

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:35.920
So Hank, today I'm going to look back at those
eight goals, and then maybe on Friday you

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:38.200
can look forward to the new ones for us.

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:43.270
Okay, Goal Number One: Between 1990 and 2015,
cut in half the proportion of people living

00:00:43.270 --> 00:00:45.909
in extreme poverty and suffering from hunger.

00:00:45.909 --> 00:00:49.539
That doesn't seem possible to do in 25 years,
except that WE DID IT!

00:00:49.539 --> 00:00:50.539
YES, PEOPLE.

00:00:50.539 --> 00:00:55.730
So in 1990, about 1.9 billion people lived
on the equivalent of less than $1.25 per day.

00:00:55.730 --> 00:01:00.710
Now, even after adjusting for inflation etc.,
that number is more than a billion people

00:01:00.710 --> 00:01:01.710
smaller.

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:05.660
Most of this is due to huge decreases in poverty
in China and South Asia and South America,

00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:09.530
but the rate of poverty in Sub-Saharan Africa
has also fallen since 1990.

00:01:09.530 --> 00:01:13.190
As for hunger, we're probably going to miss
that goal of reducing it by 50 percent, but

00:01:13.190 --> 00:01:14.340
just barely.

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:17.350
In 1990, 23 percent of people in the developing
world were undernourished.

00:01:17.350 --> 00:01:19.490
Today, it's under 13 percent.

00:01:19.490 --> 00:01:22.590
Goal Number Two: Achieve Universal Primary
Education.

00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:24.220
They weren't messing around with these goals,
Hank.

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:26.820
Well, we're not quite there, but again, the
progress is really impressive.

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:30.500
Primary school enrollment in the developing
world is over 90 percent, and in the last

00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:35.330
25 years the global literacy rate among young
people has increased from 83 percent to 91

00:01:35.330 --> 00:01:36.330
percent.

00:01:36.330 --> 00:01:37.660
Goal Number Three: Gender Equality.

00:01:37.660 --> 00:01:42.160
Okay, so speaking of kids in schools, in South
Asia in 1990 there were 75 girls in school

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:47.360
for every 100 boys, and today there are 103
girls in school for every 100 boys.

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:48.630
Step it up, boys!

00:01:48.630 --> 00:01:52.000
Worldwide, women now make up 41 percent of
the non-agricultural workforce.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:54.000
In 1990, it was just 35 percent.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:56.640
And there are more women working in universities
and serving in parliaments.

00:01:56.640 --> 00:02:00.500
That said, there are still massive disparities
between men and women, so I hope that's a

00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:02.210
big focus for the new round of global goals.

00:02:02.210 --> 00:02:07.570
Okay, Goal Number Four: Reduce the childhood
mortality rate by 66 percent between 1990

00:02:07.570 --> 00:02:08.570
and 2015.

00:02:08.570 --> 00:02:11.291
So as you can see in this video I stole from
the Gates Foundation (sorry Bill and Melinda),

00:02:11.291 --> 00:02:16.069
a lot of kids died before the age of five
around the world in 1990, but in the last

00:02:16.069 --> 00:02:19.430
25 years, the childhood mortality rate has
dropped dramatically.

00:02:19.430 --> 00:02:24.439
Just to cite one example, measles vaccinations
have prevented about 15 million child deaths

00:02:24.439 --> 00:02:25.830
in the last 25 years.

00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:30.010
Unfortunately we're going to miss that 66
percent target reduction, but even so, global

00:02:30.010 --> 00:02:34.050
childhood mortality has dropped by more than
half since 1990.

00:02:34.050 --> 00:02:38.239
Goal Number Five: Reduce the maternal mortality
rate by 75 percent.

00:02:38.239 --> 00:02:40.870
As you can see in this Gates Foundation chart
(cool videos in the dooblydoo, by the way),

00:02:40.870 --> 00:02:45.170
there was very little progress on this front
until 2005, and then when worldwide investment

00:02:45.170 --> 00:02:50.160
in maternal health increased, maternal mortality
decreased dramatically.

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:54.300
Goal Number Six: Halt and begin to reverse
the spread of HIV/AIDS and the incidence of

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:55.300
malaria.

00:02:55.300 --> 00:02:56.340
This has been a huge success.

00:02:56.340 --> 00:03:01.120
Again, the HIV death rate began to decline
when investment in HIV prevention and treatment

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:02.139
began to increase.

00:03:02.139 --> 00:03:05.639
And we see the same thing with malaria, where
global deaths have decreased about 20 percent

00:03:05.639 --> 00:03:06.659
since 2000.

00:03:06.659 --> 00:03:10.159
Goal Number Seven: Ensure environmental sustainability.

00:03:10.159 --> 00:03:12.049
Let's not pretend that this has gone well.

00:03:12.049 --> 00:03:15.849
Goal Number Eight: "Develop a global partnership
for development."

00:03:15.849 --> 00:03:16.920
Hank, I'm going to level with you.

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:20.579
I don't know the meaning of a sentence that
seeks to develop a development.

00:03:20.579 --> 00:03:24.730
Overall, though, Hank, this has been a really
good 25 years for humans.

00:03:24.730 --> 00:03:29.629
You are much less likely to be poor or hungry
or illiterate than you were in 1990.

00:03:29.629 --> 00:03:32.669
If you're a girl, you're more likely to go
to school, and if you're a mother you're less

00:03:32.669 --> 00:03:35.859
likely to die during childbirth, or to have
to bury one of your kids.

00:03:35.859 --> 00:03:38.169
And the Millennium Development Goals had a
lot to do with this.

00:03:38.169 --> 00:03:41.840
Now they certainly weren't the only factor,
but the attention and investment that resulted

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:44.160
from the MDGs made a huge difference.

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:47.980
And that's why it's so important for all of
us to pay attention to the new goals.

00:03:47.980 --> 00:03:51.349
Hank, there are young people watching this
video who may be among the first humans to

00:03:51.349 --> 00:03:54.690
see a world where extreme poverty and hunger
are rare.

00:03:54.690 --> 00:03:58.109
Hank, I hope we can learn more about what
the plan is to get humans to that place when

00:03:58.109 --> 00:03:59.340
I see you on Friday.

